        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <span style="font-size: 14px;"><b>Objective:</b></span><div><span style="font-size: 14px;">

NASA’s priority on the Artemis missions as a preliminary phase for Mars exploration, it is expected that long-distance space exploration will propose various challenges due to the prolonged constraint of small teams living and working together in an intense environment and the absence of real-time communication with Earth, broadening the risk of inadequate team cooperation and coordination.

This proposed study aims tested the hypothesis, that a minimal set of speech-based measures, potentially different depending on the structured or unstructured nature of the focal tasks, will be indicative of team functioning degradation, and that the various parts of the mission will contribute differently to this outcome, by building a speech-based artificial intelligence system that unobtrusively predicts degradation in team functioning, including performance and cohesion, during HERA Campaign 4 (C4) and Campaign 5 (C5). The AI system conducted automated analysis of prosodic and linguistic of speech, modeled their interaction among team members, and extracted influence and turn-taking statistics among speakers, without requiring additional human coding, thus resulting in an automated method that relies on unobtrusive passive monitoring for predicting team outcomes.

<br></span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;"><br></span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;">The proposed research had three specific aims:

<br></span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;">1. Pre-process audio recordings from C4/C5 HERA missions, and extract team interaction measures based on acoustic and linguistic features from these missions.

<br></span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;">2. Design machine learning models that automatically predict degradation in team functioning using the speech measures.

<br></span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;">3. Identify a minimal subset of speech-based measures that are effective predictors of team functioning degradation for each part of the mission and specific parts of the mission that mostly contribute to team functioning degradation.
<br></span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;">
<b><br></b></span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;"><b>Approach:</b></span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;">
The PI team investigated the broad picture of team functioning throughout the whole duration of focal parts of the mission via observing structured and unstructured team interactions. Audio data were pre-processed via removing stationary and short burst noise, followed by voice activity detection and speaker identification.

Speaker excerpts were transcribed via automatic speech recognition (ASR), where speaker adaptation was applied to improve ASR performance. The following acoustic and linguistic features were extracted: degree of speech periodicity (e.g., fundamental frequency-F0, jitter, shimmer); shape of the speech spectrum (e.g., cepstrum, Mel-frequency spectral coefficients); patterns of vocal intensity (e.g., loudness); grammatic features (e.g., I/you/we pronouns); and lexicon-based features capturing cognitive, affective, and social processes.

Interaction measures included the degree of similarity in acoustic and linguistic features among interlocutors; the degree of connectivity of a graph, team member nodes and edges; and turn-taking measures modeling the conversation progress (e.g., alternating turns, pauses, speech overlap).

Interpretable machine learning (ML) models were trained based on the interaction measures to predict self- reported team performance and coherence. Ablation studies were conducted to identify the subset of features, potentially different depending on the structured or unstructured

nature of the focal tasks, and parts of the mission that are most predictive of team functioning, degradation, and feasible prediction horizon windows.

Investigators used previously collected self-reports, audio, and team task performances from structured and unstructured tasks from the HERA Campaigns 4 and 5. Acoustic and linguistic measurements were used for the outputs of team functioning variables when training ML models. To better understand data and feasible system configurations, various trainings and testing occurred. Team-independent evaluation was conducted via a leave-one-team-out cross-validation, where data from 8 teams was used for training and data from the remaining team were used for testing.

Team-dependent evaluation was conducted such that a subset of data from a target team was included in the training set and the remaining were in the test set. To increase the model’s robustness and generalizability investigators included data from other teams. Longitudinal data was used for multiple horizons prediction (1-5 days in advance). Once investigators aggregate the data at the day level, employ were implemented for multi-level models for hypothesis testing.
<br></span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;"><br></span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;">
<b>Results:</b></span></div><div><span style="font-size: 14px;">
The results from this investigation allowed researchers to isolate and record only the important vocal behaviors during specific parts of the mission, instead of the entire mission. They also examined the predictive ability of speech-based interaction measures without additional sensor devices (e.g., badges for crew proximity). The reduced need for human coding will benefit future missions and crew by being both an automated, unobtrusive monitoring system for predicting team outcomes. 
</span><div><br></div></div>
